The strongest upgrade is not adding another agent framework. Your explicit 10-phase pipeline is already a sensible architecture.

What you need now is an evaluation-driven control layer around it:

* Typed contracts between phases.
* Repeatable benchmarks for every prompt/model combination.
* Full traces rather than only aggregate token counts.
* Automatic cheap-model routing with validation and fallback.
* Deterministic verification of generated migrations.

One terminology improvement: separate agent_profile from model. The agent defines instructions, tools and permissions; the model determines capability, speed and cost.

Recommended stack

Priority	Technology	What it adds
1	GitHub Copilot Python SDK	Replaces fragile subprocess/stdout handling
2	Pydantic contracts	Machine-validatable phase outputs
3	Promptfoo	Prompt/model A/B testing and CI regression tests
4	OpenTelemetry + MLflow	Complete traces, prompt versions and experiment history
5	Validation-based model router	Cheapest model that reliably passes
6	Ragas/Docling	Better document ingestion and retrieval evaluation
7	ast-grep/Tree-sitter/Hypothesis	Deterministic migration and stronger verification
8	DSPy/GEPA	Automatic prompt optimization—after the eval dataset exists

1. Replace the raw subprocess boundary

GitHub’s biggest recent change is directly relevant to your project: the Copilot SDK became generally available in June 2026. The Python package, github-copilot-sdk, controls the same runtime as Copilot CLI through JSON-RPC. It handles runtime compatibility, sessions, streaming events, tools, permissions, hooks and OpenTelemetry. ⁠￼

Keep your orchestration and introduce an adapter:

class AgentBackend(Protocol):
    async def run_phase(
        self,
        phase: PhaseSpec,
        inputs: dict,
    ) -> PhaseResult: ...

Initially implement CopilotSdkBackend; retain your subprocess implementation temporarily as a fallback. This prevents GitHub-specific details from leaking into the orchestration.

The SDK exposes per-model-call:

* Input, output, reasoning and cached tokens.
* Duration.
* Premium-request cost.
* Subagent attribution.
* Context-window utilization.
* Session-level AI-credit limits.

That is much more accurate than parsing the final CLI statistics. The live usage events are ephemeral, so persist them when received. ⁠￼

2. Turn every phase boundary into a contract

Do not let one phase pass an arbitrary Markdown essay to the next phase. Define Pydantic models such as:

class Evidence(BaseModel):
    source_id: str
    location: str
    content_hash: str
class MigrationRequirement(BaseModel):
    requirement_id: str
    description: str
    evidence: list[Evidence]
    confidence: float
class MigrationPlan(BaseModel):
    requirements: list[MigrationRequirement]
    operations: list[MigrationOperation]
    validation_commands: list[str]
    expected_changed_files: list[str]

With the Copilot SDK, you can expose something like submit_phase_result as a typed custom tool generated from a Pydantic model. The agent must call it once; your Python code validates the arguments.

If validation fails:

1. Return the exact validation errors.
2. Retry once using the same model.
3. If it still fails, use the stronger fallback model.
4. Stop rather than silently passing malformed output downstream.

This will improve reliability more than almost any fashionable orchestration library.

3. Add a real prompt/model benchmark with Promptfoo

Promptfoo is the most immediately applicable open-source tool for your “which prompt or model actually succeeds?” question. It can compare prompts and models across a test matrix, use deterministic or LLM-based assertions, cache results, run concurrently and fail CI on regressions. Most importantly, its custom Python provider can invoke your existing phase runner or entire migration pipeline. ⁠￼

Build a golden dataset of approximately 30–50 representative migrations:

* Straightforward migration.
* Missing or contradictory documentation.
* Large repository.
* Already partially migrated.
* Deprecated APIs.
* Generated code.
* Unsupported constructs.
* Cases from actual previous failures.

For each candidate, record:

Metric	Purpose
Hard success	Build and tests pass
Requirement coverage	All required changes implemented
Unsupported claims	Summary stays grounded in documents
Idempotence	Second migration produces no additional diff
Unwanted changes	Files outside the expected scope
Tokens/credits	Cost
Wall time/p95 latency	Speed
Retry rate	Operational reliability

Run nondeterministic candidates several times. A model scoring 19/20 once is not necessarily more reliable than one scoring 56/60.

Most importantly, do not create one weighted score where low latency can compensate for incorrect code. Use:

1. Hard correctness threshold.
2. Then minimize cost.
3. Then minimize latency.

4. Add end-to-end tracing

Use one root span per migration and one child span per phase. Record:

phase
agent_profile
model_id
prompt_version
prompt_hash
input_hash
document_snapshot_hash
repository_commit
attempt
tokens_in/out
cached_tokens
premium_cost
latency
validator_results
changed_file_count
final_status

My default choice would be self-hosted MLflow 3.x. It accepts OpenTelemetry traces over OTLP, provides prompt versioning, datasets, evaluation experiments and prompt comparison. Its prompt-evaluation UI can compare prompt versions across models and datasets. ⁠￼

Langfuse is the main alternative if you prefer a more agent-specific interface; it combines traces, prompt management, datasets, experiments and online/offline evaluation. Do not deploy MLflow, Langfuse and Phoenix together initially—the overlap is substantial. ⁠￼

For bank repositories, disable content capture by default. Store hashes and operational metadata, then enable prompt/output capture only in a sanitized evaluation environment.

5. Make model selection empirical

Your config can evolve toward:

phases:
  create_migration_plan:
    agent_profile: migration-planner
    prompt: migration-planner@v7
    model_policy:
      candidates: [cheap, balanced]
      fallback: strong
      minimum_pass_rate: 0.98
    output_contract: MigrationPlan
    validators:
      - schema
      - requirement_coverage
      - source_grounding

Routing policy:

* Select the cheapest model whose measured reliability meets the phase threshold.
* On a validation failure, retry with the failure information.
* Escalate to the stronger model only if necessary.
* Evaluate new models in shadow benchmark runs before routing real work to them.
* Keep logical aliases such as cheap, balanced, and strong, but record the resolved model ID.

This transforms model selection from opinion into measured policy.

6. Improve the document phase separately

If your documents include PDF, DOCX, PowerPoint or complex HTML, Docling is worth testing. It runs locally and converts these formats into structured Markdown/JSON while retaining headings, reading order and tables. ⁠￼

For retrieval:

* Combine lexical search/BM25 with embeddings.
* Rerank the top results with a cross-encoder.
* Preserve source location and content hash in every extracted requirement.
* Evaluate retrieval separately from summarization.

Ragas provides context precision, context recall and agent/RAG-oriented metrics, but only add it if your “fetch documents” phase is genuinely doing retrieval. ⁠￼

7. Make the migration less dependent on generated free-form code

Where possible:

* Let the LLM identify transformations and generate rules.
* Apply repeatable transformations using ast-grep, Tree-sitter or a language-specific codemod library.
* Use the LLM for exceptional cases that cannot be expressed structurally.

ast-grep provides polyglot AST-aware searching and rewriting; Tree-sitter provides parsers across many languages. Hypothesis can generate edge cases and shrink failures to minimal examples. ⁠￼

Your final code gate should ideally include:

* Compilation.
* Existing tests.
* Migration-specific contract tests.
* Static analysis.
* Forbidden-pattern scan.
* Idempotence test.
* Source-versus-target behavioural comparison where feasible.

8. Automatic prompt optimization comes last

Once the benchmark and metrics are trustworthy, experiment with DSPy’s MIPROv2 or GEPA. These generate alternative instructions/few-shot examples and optimize them against your metric. MLflow also exposes GEPA-based prompt optimization. ⁠￼

Do not start here. Without a representative dataset and reliable metric, an optimizer merely learns to exploit weaknesses in your evaluator.

My concrete implementation order would be:

1. Copilot SDK adapter.
2. Pydantic phase contracts.
3. OpenTelemetry phase traces.
4. Golden migration dataset and Promptfoo matrix.
5. Validation-triggered model fallback.
6. Exact content-addressed caching.
7. Retrieval evaluation and structural codemods.
8. GEPA/DSPy experimentation.

I would not add LangGraph merely to make the project appear more agentic. Your workflow is largely deterministic and phase-based; explicit Python remains easier to test. Add Temporal only if migrations must survive process crashes, machine restarts or multi-hour distributed execution.

